From 04c7b9a2965c0d932702e61c547d6ca6f74ce5ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Devin <158243242+devin-ai-integration[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:08:56 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(replication): report real commit-to-apply lag, not
 LSN-diff*10
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/v1/replication/status disagreed with itself: the leader view and the
follower ack loop both computed lag as (LSN gap)*10 ms — a count scaled
by an assumed 10ms/entry, not a time — while the follower apply path
measured real entry-timestamp age. The two writers raced on the same
field, so self-reported lag oscillated between definitions and the
leader under-reported real lag ~4x (MEM-0799: leader saw ~4.6s
"warning" while the follower measured ~93-238s real).

One definition everywhere now: lag = wall-clock age of the follower's
applied tip, 0 at the leader's head so idle leaders don't read as lag.

- binlog: bounded LSN->commit-ts index (16384 slots), populated on
  append and backfilled by recoverLSN, so lag lookups never scan.
- leader (HTTP + gRPC status): lag_ms = now - commit_ts(confirmed tip)
  via TimestampAt; predating the index reports the index-floor age as
  a lower bound.
- follower: lagMs computed on read from lastAppliedCommitTs; ackLoop
  records leaderHeadLSN for the at-head check; the LSN-gap estimate
  survives only as the pre-first-apply fallback.
- follower: heartbeat send-timestamps now measure leader-local clock
  offset; commit-ts lag is computed in leader time so host clock skew
  cannot inflate or hide it. Exported as leader_clock_offset_ms.
- new lag_lsn on both views exposes the raw LSN gap the old estimate
  was derived from.
---
